<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wat Phra Kaew</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Temple of the Emerald Buddha</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wat Phra Kaew, the Temple of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, is the royal temple within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a>. Built by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> from 1782 and consecrated in 1784, it houses the Emerald Buddha, the most venerated image in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, whose robes the king changes with the seasons three times a year. Its gilded buildings, guardian giants and murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a> make it the foremost Buddhist temple of the country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wat-phra-kaew.jpg" alt="Wat Phra Kaew" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original image: Ninara from Helsinki, Finland. Edit: TSP · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wat_Phra_Kaew_by_Ninara_TSP_edit_crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>When King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangkok">Bangkok</a> the capital of the Rattanakosin Kingdom on 6 April 1782, the new Chakri dynasty needed a royal palace and temple to legitimise it. He wished to distance himself from the regime of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">King Taksin</a>, whom he had replaced, and the old palace at Thonburi was small and hemmed in between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-arun">Wat Arun</a> and Wat Tai Talat. The temple stands within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a> precincts. Each later king has added to, restored or embellished it as a way of making religious merit and glorifying the dynasty, and has donated sacred objects to its treasury.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerald-buddha">Emerald Buddha</a>, the temple's namesake, is a meditating Buddha in the lotus position, about 66 centimetres tall, of a green semi-precious stone usually described as jade and clothed in gold. It sits on a pedestal at the western end of the ordination hall, the Ubosot, which is ringed by forty-eight columns covered in glass mosaic and by eight pavilions with gilded boundary stones. Its murals depict the life of the Buddha, the Buddha's victory over Mara and Buddhist cosmology.</p><p>A raised terrace carries the Phra Mondop, a library for the Tripitaka that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> had revised in 1788 after the sack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> in 1767; the Phra Si Rattana Chedi, a gilded stupa holding relics from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>; and the Royal Pantheon, rebuilt under Rama VI after a fire in 1903 and opened each year on 6 April, Chakri Memorial Day. Rama IV had a scale model of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-wat">Angkor Wat</a> made after that temple was rediscovered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Important state and royal ceremonies are held in the temple each year, presided over by the king in person and attended by government officials, which makes it a national shrine for the monarchy and the state. A covered gallery around the complex carries murals of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramakien">Ramakien</a>, the Thai version of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, in 178 scenes; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-i">Rama I</a> had the epic recomposed in Thai verse around 1797. The murals were repainted under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Rama III</a> and have been restored often. Twelve guardian giants, each about 5 metres tall and drawn from the Ramakien, stand at the gates.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wat Phra Kaew” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Grand Palace and <b>Wat Phra Kaew</b> rose at its centre, and in 1784 the Emerald Buddha, which Chakri himself had brought from Vientiane in 1779, was installed there as the protective image of the realm.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Its scenes appear in terracottas, stone sculptures, bronzes and paintings, from the 3rd-century panel at Nagarjunakonda to the 9th-century reliefs of Prambanan in Java and the murals of <b>Wat Phra Kaew</b> in Bangkok.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
